= Information theory =

== Overview ==
'''Information theory''' is the mathematical study of quantifying, coding, transmitting, and processing information under uncertainty and noise.

It was formalized by Claude Shannon in the context of communication systems.

== Core quantities ==
Major concepts include:

* [[Entropy]];
* conditional entropy;
* mutual information;
* relative entropy;
* source coding;
* channel capacity;
* rate–distortion tradeoffs.

== Communication model ==
A communication system can be idealized as:

<pre>
source → encoder → channel → decoder → destination
</pre>

Noise in the channel can corrupt transmitted symbols.

Information theory studies the limits of reliable representation and communication.

== Semantics ==
Shannon's formalism intentionally abstracts away from message meaning.

Two messages with different semantic importance can have the same Shannon information statistics.

Thus:

<pre>
information theory ≠ theory of semantics
</pre>

== Coding theorems ==
Shannon's source-coding theorem relates entropy to the minimum average lossless description length.

The channel-coding theorem identifies communication rates below channel capacity for which arbitrarily low error can be approached under suitable coding assumptions.

== Related concepts ==
[[Information]], [[Entropy]], [[Probability]], [[Probability distribution]], [[Conditional probability]], [[Correlation]], and [[Uncertainty]] provide the mathematical context.

